# NeuPerm payload provenance manifest
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
# -----------------
# The NeuPerm SNR evaluation (paper Section VIII-B, Table 5, Figure 3)
# was computed on nine real malware samples, obfuscated on disk as XOR-with-0xFF
# encoded files. Those live samples are NOT distributed with this artifact; they
# are replaced by safe, deterministic synthetic substitutes under
# `payloads/synthetic/`.
#
# This manifest records, for each of the nine original samples, the cryptographic
# hashes that uniquely identify the exact bytes the published numbers were
# computed on. It distributes NO malware: a SHA-256 is a one-way fingerprint, not
# the sample. It gives an authorised researcher everything needed to obtain the
# exact originals and reproduce Table 5 / Figure 3 to the decimal.
#
# HASH DEFINITIONS
# ----------------
#   xor_sha256   : SHA-256 of the stored bytes (each byte = plaintext ^ 0xFF),
#                  i.e. the obfuscated on-disk form of the original sample.
#   plain_sha256 : SHA-256 of the deobfuscated sample (stored ^ 0xFF). This is
#                  the SHA-256 of the actual malware PE, and the value an
#                  authorised researcher matches against a malware repository.
#   length       : exact byte length of the sample (identical for both forms).
#   entropy      : Shannon entropy (bits/byte) of the sample. XOR with a constant
#                  is a byte-wise bijection, so the stored and deobfuscated forms
#                  share one entropy value.
#
# The obfuscation key was verified in memory, not assumed: deobfuscating each
# member with 0xFF yields a valid `MZ` DOS/PE header, confirming XOR-0xFF.
# The malware bytes were never written to disk during this process.
#
# HOW AN AUTHORISED RESEARCHER OBTAINS THE ORIGINALS
# --------------------------------------------------
# These are well-known samples from public malware corpora (e.g. theZoo,
# github.com/ytisf/theZoo, archive password `infected`; VirusShare; MalwareBazaar
# by abuse.ch). Retrieve each sample by its `plain_sha256` below through your
# institution's authorised malware-research channel, XOR-encode with 0xFF if you
# wish to reproduce the exact on-disk `.xor` form, place the files in the
# directory your run points `MALWARE_DIR` / `SNR_PAYLOADS` at, and re-run the
# Section VIII-B SNR scripts. Distribution of the live samples is intentionally
# out of scope for this artifact.
#
# SUBSTITUTES
# -----------
# `payloads/synthetic/` ships nine inert substitutes, one per sample, generated
# by `payloads/synthetic/generate_substitutes.py` (seed=42). Each matches its
# original's length exactly and its Shannon entropy closely, carries a
# SYNTHETIC-NEUPERM banner, and contains no PE/ELF header. Their hashes are
# listed in the SUBSTITUTES section so the shipped artifact is itself verifiable.
# A few published numbers depend on payload CONTENT rather than only payload
# LENGTH; those specific cells require the original samples to reproduce exactly.

# ORIGINALS
